meta {
  name: OPA-external
}

docs {
  # OPA-external — the external decision contract

  How an external consumer (a gateway / integration platform, e.g. MuleSoft) asks
  OPA for an authorization decision.

  **Endpoint:** `POST {{opaUrl}}/v1/data/umzh/authz/gateway/allow`

  **You send** the FHIR request you are proxying + the client's bearer — nothing
  else:

  ```json
  { "input": { "request": {
      "method": "GET",
      "path": "/fhir/Task",
      "query": {},
      "headers": { "authorization": "Bearer <token>" }
  } } }
  ```

  **You get** `{ "result": true }` (allow) or `{ "result": false }` (deny).

  ## What OPA actually reads from your token

  OPA does **not** verify the signature (the consumer authenticates the token
  first) — it **decodes** the bearer and reads three claims. A decoded access-token
  payload looks like this:

  ```json
  {
    "iss": "https://auth.umzhconnect.ch/realms/umzh-connect",
    "sub": "fulfiller-client-l2",
    "aud": "https://auth.umzhconnect.ch/realms/umzh-connect/protocol/openid-connect/token",
    "exp": 1730000000,

    "scope": "system/Task.crus system/ServiceRequest.r system/Patient.r",
    "extensions": {
      "umzhconnect": {
        "organization_reference": "https://registry.umzhconnect.ch/fhir/Organization/HospitalB"
      }
    },
    "fhirContext": [
      { "reference": "ServiceRequest/sr-e2e" }
    ]
  }
  ```

  Only the **last three** drive the decision:

  | Claim | Used for |
  |---|---|
  | `scope` | SMART scopes, space-delimited; combined letters are fine, e.g. `system/Task.crus` (= c+r+u+s) |
  | `extensions.umzhconnect.organization_reference` | the caller's org — matched against the Consent actor and Task requester/owner |
  | `fhirContext` | the ServiceRequest / Task graph(s) the caller is consented for |

  `iss` / `aud` / `exp` / `sub` are for the consumer's own **authentication** step,
  not OPA's decision. You pass **no** `fhir_base`, no scope, no org separately —
  they come from the token (and, for `fhir_base`, OPA's own config).

  ## Getting a bearer

  Run the **Auth** folder (*Get token*) to mint one,
  then copy the resulting `accessToken` into the `bearerToken` env var — or paste
  any token from your auth server. Its scope / org / fhirContext decide the
  outcome. The graph/consent requests (Task update, ServiceRequest search, read by
  id) also need the stack up + seeded data (`tests/scripts/seed.sh up`).
}
